>> A8: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

We chose to implement access to user memory by first verifying that the pointer passed into the system call is a valid user address and has an entry on the PintOS page directory (inside of 'test_address ()'), then dereferencing that memory address into the arguments for the given system call. We did this because before we started writing our implementation, that made the most logical sense for us as we just needed to verify the address passed in was less than PHYS_BASE ('is_user_vaddr ()'), and make sure that it has been mapped on the page directory (functions from userprog/pagedir.c and threads/vaddr.h). Further we ensure that the whole buffer doesn't leak into kernel memory or unmapped regions with the call to 'test_address_range ()' which moves through the buffer in 4KB steps.


>> A9: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages: The underlying data structure is very simple and straightforward. It's a fixed size, so we don't have to worry about dynamic allocations. Lookups happen in constant time since the underlying data structure is just an array. Additionally, each process maintains their own file descriptor table, which enforces isolation between processes.

Disadvantages: Each process can only open up to 128 files. From a PintOS perspective, this is fine, but in a practical OS, this might be a harsh limit. Also, this implementation doesn't keep track of references, which means the kernel has no idea how many processes are still using a file at a given time. This is a problem because we might inadvertently close a file or re-enable write permissions to an executable too early. While these issues aren't much of a concern for PintOS, for an actual OS, these would be very important considerations.